//? Title: Early Stopping and Checkpoints: Selection Is Not Resume
//? Subtitle: Validation decisions, best-state restoration and deterministic continuation
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Training loss can keep improving while the quantity used to select a model gets worse.
We need an explicit stopping rule and a way to restore the selected state. A separate
problem is resuming the same optimization trajectory: weights alone omit momentum,
adaptive moments and counters. Small deterministic examples expose patience boundaries,
restore a best scalar model, and compare full-state versus weights-only Adam continuation.
All archives and figures are saved locally; no population generalization claim is made.
)
\toc

> The method


EarlyStopping observes a finite metric at strictly increasing nonnegative epoch indices.
For minimization it accepts a new best only when

$$
 m_t<m_{best}-\delta.
$$
\alttext(A minimizing validation metric must improve by strictly more than min delta.)

The first observation becomes best, a strict improvement resets the miss count, and
other observations increment it. A non-improvement stops when misses reach patience;
patience zero still waits for the first non-improving observation. Maximization reverses
the inequality. Epoch gaps do not count extra misses: this is a count of observations,
not elapsed epochs. The monitor records a decision but does not itself save a model.

Checkpoint-best selection and exact resume preserve different state. The checkpoint
helpers can save ParameterStore weights, Optimizer slots/counter and optionally BatchOrder
permutation/cursor/RNG. They do not serialize the whole program: recreate compatible
parameter creation order, optimizer configuration, rate/schedule and batching settings.
Application RNGs, other model state and the stopping monitor must be persisted separately
when needed. Validation repeatedly used for stopping is selection data, not an untouched
final test set. Restore into existing parameter handles before computing a new loss.

The examples have no dropout, random operations or sampled batches; exact agreement here
is conditional on that deterministic setup and a compatible environment. No dataset is
needed for a controlled stopping decision or a state-continuation check.


The concept diagram is generated from the retained local TikZ source. LaTeX with
PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm, is needed only to regenerate it.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/early_stopping_checkpoints_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/early_stopping_checkpoints_concept.tikz > build/early_stopping_checkpoints.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/early_stopping_checkpoints_concept.svg build/early_stopping_checkpoints_concept.dvi > build/early_stopping_checkpoints.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered build/early_stopping_checkpoints_concept.svg from local TikZ source.\n'
```
