> Simple examples

>> A five-observation patience boundary

Minimize the metrics (1,.95,.8,.8,.85) with patience two and min_delta .1. The .95 value is too small an improvement; .8 is accepted; the final tie/worsening pair exhausts patience. Ties do not improve the best.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/early_decisions.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::nn::EarlyStopping     monitor(2, .1);
  const std::vector<double> metric = {1, .95, .8, .8, .85};
  std::vector<double>       epoch, best, misses, improved, stop;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < metric.size(); ++i) {
    const auto d = monitor.observe(metric[i], i);
    epoch.push_back(i);
    best.push_back(monitor.best());
    misses.push_back(monitor.misses());
    improved.push_back(d.improved);
    stop.push_back(d.stop);
  }
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame t;
  t.add_column("epoch", epoch);
  t.add_column("metric", metric);
  t.add_column("best", best);
  t.add_column("misses", misses);
  t.add_column("improved", improved);
  t.add_column("stop", stop);
  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.lines(epoch, metric, "observed", {37, 99, 235})
      .lines(epoch, best, "accepted best", {220, 38, 38})
      .title("Strict improvement and two consecutive misses")
      .x_label("observation epoch")
      .y_label("validation metric")
      .y_limits(.7, 1.2);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  p.size(900, 420).save_svg("build/early_decisions.svg");
  std::print("{}\n", t.to_md(3));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| epoch | metric | best | misses | improved | stop |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 1.00 | 1.0 | 0 | 1 | 0 |
| 1 | 0.95 | 1.0 | 1 | 0 | 0 |
| 2 | 0.80 | 0.8 | 0 | 1 | 0 |
| 3 | 0.80 | 0.8 | 1 | 0 | 0 |
| 4 | 0.85 | 0.8 | 2 | 0 | 1 |

\image(figures/early_decisions.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Patience is reset only by an accepted improvement; min_delta is a strict threshold.)
\alttext(Observed metric and accepted-best curves are shown across five observations. The table includes each improvement flag, miss count and stopping decision.)

>> A training target and selection target deliberately disagree

Use training loss (w−4)² and validation loss (w−2)² with SGD rate .1.
Starting at zero, the exact update is w_k=4(1−.8^k). Training loss decreases, but
validation reaches its discrete best at update three. A patience-three monitor stops
three misses later. This is a designed conflict between two targets, not evidence of
statistical overfitting on a real dataset. The checkpoint contains weights and counter.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/early_best.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::nn::ParameterStore store(0, dm::nn::DType::Float64);
  auto                   w = store.get("w", {}, dm::nn::init::constant(0));
  dm::nn::Optimizer      opt(store, .1);
  dm::nn::EarlyStopping  monitor(3);
  std::vector<double>    update, training, validation;
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  for (int k = 1; k <= 30; ++k) {
    opt.minimize(dm::nn::square(w - 4.));
    const double a = w.item<double>();
    update.push_back(k);
    training.push_back((a - 4) * (a - 4));
    validation.push_back((a - 2) * (a - 2));
    const auto d = monitor.observe(validation.back(), k);
    if (d.improved)
      dm::nn::save_training_checkpoint("build/early_best.npz", store, &opt);
    if (d.stop)
      break;
  }
  const double last = w.item<double>();
  dm::nn::load_training_checkpoint("build/early_best.npz", store, &opt);
  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.lines(update, training, "training target 4", {37, 99, 235})
      .lines(update, validation, "validation target 2", {220, 38, 38})
      .points(
          {double(monitor.best_epoch())}, {monitor.best()}, "selected checkpoint", {22, 163, 74}, 6)
      .title("Training improves after validation turns")
      .x_label("optimizer update")
      .y_label("squared target error")
      .y_limits(0, 14);
  p.size(900, 430).save_svg("build/early_best.svg");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame t;
  t.add_column("last_weight", std::vector<double>{last});
  t.add_column("restored_weight", std::vector<double>{w.item<double>()});
  t.add_column("restored_step", std::vector<double>{double(opt.iterations())});
  t.add_column("best_validation", std::vector<double>{monitor.best()});
  std::print("{}\n", t.to_md(9));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| last_weight | restored_weight | restored_step | best_validation |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 2.951424 | 1.952 | 3 | 0.002304 |

\image(figures/early_best.svg)
// result_end
\caption(A best-state checkpoint restores the selected weight and optimizer counter after later updates have worsened the selection metric.)
\alttext(Training error decreases while validation error first decreases and then increases. A marker identifies the selected checkpoint; a table compares last and restored weights and counter.)

> Checking the theory

>> A full Adam checkpoint reproduces continuation

Save after 17 updates toward the scalar training target four. Continue for 23 updates,
then restore into fresh compatible Adam instances and repeat those 23 updates. One
instance loads all optimizer state; the other loads only weights. Constant rate .1 is
recreated explicitly. The program checks every serialized optimizer value and the weight
against uninterrupted continuation. The weights-only comparison does not preserve the
counter or Adam moments and therefore answers a different continuation question.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/early_resume.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::nn::ParameterStore a(0, dm::nn::DType::Float64), b(0, dm::nn::DType::Float64),
      c(0, dm::nn::DType::Float64);
  auto wa = a.get("w", {}, dm::nn::init::constant(0)),
       wb = b.get("w", {}, dm::nn::init::constant(99)),
       wc = c.get("w", {}, dm::nn::init::constant(99));
  const dm::nn::OptimizerConfig cfg{dm::nn::OptimizerKind::Adam};
  dm::nn::Optimizer             oa(a, .1, cfg), ob(b, .1, cfg), oc(c, .1, cfg);
  for (int i = 0; i < 17; ++i)
    oa.minimize(dm::nn::square(wa - 4.));
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::nn::save_training_checkpoint("build/early_resume.npz", a, &oa);
  for (int i = 0; i < 23; ++i)
    oa.minimize(dm::nn::square(wa - 4.));
  dm::nn::load_training_checkpoint("build/early_resume.npz", b, &ob);
  dm::nn::load_training_checkpoint("build/early_resume.npz", c);
  for (int i = 0; i < 23; ++i) {
    ob.minimize(dm::nn::square(wb - 4.));
    oc.minimize(dm::nn::square(wc - 4.));
  }
  double     worst = std::abs(wa.item<double>() - wb.item<double>());
  const auto sa = oa.state(), sb = ob.state();
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < sa.size(); ++i) {
    if (sa[i].first != sb[i].first)
      throw std::runtime_error("state names differ");
    const auto x = sa[i].second.to_vector<double>(), y = sb[i].second.to_vector<double>();
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < x.size(); ++j)
      worst = std::max(worst, std::abs(x[j] - y[j]));
  }
  if (worst != 0)
    throw std::runtime_error("full checkpoint continuation differs");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame t;
  t.add_column("mode", std::vector<std::string>{"uninterrupted", "full restore", "weights only"});
  t.add_column(
      "weight", std::vector<double>{wa.item<double>(), wb.item<double>(), wc.item<double>()});
  t.add_column(
      "counter",
      std::vector<double>{
          double(oa.iterations()), double(ob.iterations()), double(oc.iterations())});
  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.bars(
       {0, 1},
       {worst, std::abs(wc.item<double>() - wa.item<double>())},
       "weight error",
       {37, 99, 235},
       .5)
      .title("Resume requires more than weights")
      .x_label("0 = full restore, 1 = weights only")
      .y_label("difference from uninterrupted weight")
      .y_limits(0, 1);
  p.size(900, 420).save_svg("build/early_resume.svg");
  std::print("{}\n\nMaximum full-state discrepancy: {:.1e}.\n", t.to_md(9), worst);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| mode | weight | counter |
| :--- | ---: | ---: |
| uninterrupted | 3.349469207 | 40 |
| full restore | 3.349469207 | 40 |
| weights only | 3.682985442 | 23 |


Maximum full-state discrepancy: 0.0e+00.
\image(figures/early_resume.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Exact deterministic resume restores optimizer moments and step as well as the model parameters. The weights-only branch intentionally restarts its optimizer.)
\alttext(A bar chart compares full-state and weights-only weight differences from uninterrupted continuation. The table reports each final weight and optimizer counter.)

> Conclusion

The stopping monitor selects observations by a declared rule; checkpoint helpers preserve
specified state components. A best validation point and an exact continuation are separate
requirements. The controlled examples expose both without conflating validation selection
with untouched-test evaluation or assuming that arbitrary training randomness is saved.

> References

- Keras authors. [EarlyStopping|https://keras.io/api/callbacks/early_stopping/]. Canonical callback terminology; this report uses the native monitor plus explicit checkpoint calls.
- Kingma, D. P. and Ba, J. (2015). [Adam: A Method for Stochastic Optimization|https://arxiv.org/abs/1412.6980]. Moment/counter state motivates the resume comparison.
